  "query_preview": "..Mostly sunny skies early this afternoon with \ntemperatures warming to about 5 to 8 degrees above normal as the \nhigh pressure is located along the Four Corners region with easterly \nflow aloft prevailing. We are seeing some build-ups starting in the \nWhite Mountains and Sky Islands along with western Pima County. \nDrier air along the NM border will tend to limit thunderstorm \nactivity in that region. Isolated to widely scattered showers and \nthunderstorms are expected later this afternoon into the evening \nhours, generally focused on Pima, Pinal and Santa Cruz Counties. \nWhile coverage will be below average, any thunderstorms that do form \nwill be capable of producing gusty winds of 40-50+ mph along with \nareas of blowing dust given the higher DCAPE values and inverted-v \nsoundings. Locally brief heavy rain is also possible but flooding \nissues into the evening should be mitigated due to lack of storm \norganization/clustering. \n\nFrom Sunday into the middle of the work week, it's looking like an \naverage to above average period for thunderstorm activity as the \nhigh which will initially be by the Four Corners drifts into New \nMexico. East-southeasterly flow will prevail during this pattern \nwith Monday and Wednesday currently looking like the more \"up\" days \nwith better upper level support. The flow at the end of the week \ninto the weekend will become more southerly to southwesterly with \nthe potential by next weekend to keep most of the thunderstorms east \nof Tucson. Still plenty of time to iron out those details. \nOtherwise, with the daily chances of thunderstorms we'll see the \ntypical hazards including wind and flooding.",
